    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mt-2">Hand</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-2">Keep a hand acupressure chart handy and self-care is always in arm's reach. LI4 (Hegu), HT7 (Shenmen), and PC8 (Laogong) sit on the thumb webbing, wrist, and palm — quick targets for headache, nausea, anxiety, and stress, with no mat or special setup.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mb-10 flex justify-center"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 140" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-32 h-36"><rect x="20" y="50" width="80" height="60" rx="12" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><rect x="10" y="20" width="18" height="45" rx="8" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1"></rect><rect x="46" y="8" width="18" height="55" rx="8" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1"></rect><rect x="82" y="18" width="18" height="45" rx="8" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1"></rect><rect x="36" y="65" width="48" height="35" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.3"></rect><circle cx="26" cy="80" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="60" cy="80" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="94" cy="80" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><text x="60" y="135" font-family="system-ui" font-size="10" fill="#2d6a4f" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Hand</text></svg></div>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Hand Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The hands are where many people first learn acupressure, precisely because the whole point set is visible on a single hand acupressure chart. LI4 (Hegu), in the fleshy web between thumb and index finger, is frequently called the master point for headache- and toothache-type discomfort in traditional practice. HT7 (Shenmen), at the little-finger side of the wrist crease, is the classic target in routines for insomnia, anxiety, and heart-centered calm. PC8 (Laogong) sits in the center of the palm and is used for stress and heat, while LU9 (Taiyuan) and SI3 (Houxi) broaden the chart to cover cough, wrist, and neck concerns. The same points reappear across our headache, nausea, sleep, and anxiety guides.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Each hand channel — Lung, Pericardium, Heart, Large Intestine, and Small Intestine — begins or ends at the fingertips, which is how classical practice reads a small area like the hand as a control surface for distant regions such as the head and chest. Physically, the hands are dense with sensory nerve endings, so point work there is felt clearly and quickly. That combination of clear sensation and wide reach makes hand points ideal for quick, discreet self-care.</p>
  </section>

