  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Hand Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Pinch the target area between the thumb and index finger of your other hand and press at ninety degrees into the most tender, fleshy point — not onto bone. Build pressure slowly over ten to fifteen seconds until you feel the deep, dull deqi ache, then hold for thirty seconds to one minute for a quick acute need, or up to two minutes when you can sit and breathe evenly. Finish by pressing the same points on the opposite hand so both sides stay balanced.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where is LI4 and how do I find it?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">LI4 (Hegu) lives in the webbing between thumb and index finger, at the highest point of the muscle when you press your thumb toward your index finger. Squeeze the webbing corner and feel for the spot that produces a deep, dull, spreading ache rather than a sharp nerve jolt — that tenderness is the reliable landmark. The area is sensitive, so ease in gradually; the goal is a heavy, releasing sensation, never pain.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can hand pressure help nausea?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">PC6 (Neiguan), just above the inner wrist, and LI4 (Hegu) on the hand webbing are the points most associated with nausea in acupressure practice, and pressing them is a common travel-sickness ritual. It is worth trying as a self-help step for queasiness, thirty to sixty seconds with even breathing, but it is not a guaranteed remedy. Nausea has many causes, so persistent, severe, or unexplained bouts deserve a clinician's opinion.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Plan thirty seconds to two minutes per hand point, repeated on both hands. For an acute moment — a coming headache or a wave of nausea — thirty to sixty seconds on LI4 can be enough. For relaxation work such as HT7 before bed, favor the one-to-two-minute hold. Trust the deqi feeling over the clock, breathe steadily, and ease off gradually.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
    <a href="/atlas/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to Body Atlas</a>
  </div>
